# Globs # {#globs}

A <dfn>glob</dfn> can be any [=string=]. It can contain any number of wildcards: `*` matches zero or more characters, and `?` matches one character.

Glob matching is case-sensitive.

Issue: The engines disagree on `?`. Chromium's `base::MatchPattern` (`base/strings/pattern.cc`, `base/strings/pattern.h:16-18`) treats a run of *k* consecutive `?` as matching **0 to k** characters, so a lone `?` matches zero or one character (`MatchPattern("", "?")` is true, `base/strings/pattern_unittest.cc:22`). Gecko's `MatchGlobCore` (`toolkit/components/extensions/MatchPattern.cpp:786-790`) compiles `?` to the regex `.`, which requires **exactly one** character. WebKit does not implement `include_globs`/`exclude_globs` matching at all (see below), so it has no `?` semantics to compare.

Issue: The engines disagree on escaping. Chromium's `base::MatchPattern` treats `\` as an escape character for the next `*` or `?` (`base/strings/pattern.cc:47-53`, `base/strings/pattern_unittest.cc:19`). Gecko has no escape syntax: a `\` in a glob is matched as a literal backslash, and there is no way to write a literal `*` or `?` (`toolkit/components/extensions/MatchPattern.cpp:767-796`). WebKit does not implement glob matching.

Issue: The engines disagree on what string is matched. Chromium matches a glob against the full URL spec, including the fragment (`extensions/common/user_script.cc:37`, `url.spec()`). Gecko matches against the full URL spec with the fragment stripped (`toolkit/components/extensions/MatchPattern.cpp:161-181`, `URLInfo::CSpec()` calls `URINoRef()`). A glob ending in a fragment, such as `*#top`, can therefore match in Chromium but never in Gecko.

<div class="example">
Given the URL `https://example.com/path?query#frag`:

- `*example.com*` matches in both Chromium and Gecko.
- `*#frag` matches in Chromium, but not in Gecko, because Gecko matches against the URL with its fragment removed.

</div>

## Key `include_globs` ## {#globs-key-include_globs}

A [=list=] of [=globs=] that a document should match. A document matches if the URL matches both the [`matches`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-content_scripts-matches) field and the `include_globs` field. If `include_globs` is empty or not present, it does not restrict matching.

Issue: WebKit does not implement `include_globs`. The manifest key is parsed and stored (`Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Extensions/WebExtension.cpp:1481-1489`), but the actual injection-matching functions never consult it and carry an explicit acknowledgment that it is missing: `// FIXME: &lt;https://webkit.org/b/246492&gt; Add support for include globs.` (`Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Extensions/WebExtension.cpp:1341`, `Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Extensions/WebExtensionContext.cpp:1202`). A content script that declares `include_globs` to narrow a broad `matches` pattern will inject on the full, unnarrowed `matches` set in WebKit-based browsers.

## Key `exclude_globs` ## {#globs-key-exclude_globs}

A [=list=] of [=globs=] that can be used to specify URLs where the content script should not run, even if the URL matches entries in [`matches`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-content_scripts-matches) and (if specified) [[#globs-key-include_globs]]. If `exclude_globs` is empty or not present, it does not exclude anything.

Issue: WebKit does not implement `exclude_globs`, for the same reason as `include_globs` above (same two FIXME sites, `WebExtension.cpp:1329` and `WebExtensionContext.cpp:1190`).
